The AI doesn't follow a strict decision tree.  Different conditions increase the change of doing something, like you mentoined def break / low hp ratio will increase the chance, while elemental disadvantage / inability effects will decrease the chance.

&nbsp;

Hitting elemental disadvantage is a really rare thing, but I've notice that ignoring elemental advantaged units is not comparatively as likely.  Which is why [Character A] will pretty much always go for [Character B], but [Character C] will once in a blue moon go for someone else.

The [character] rework looks bonkers, first thing I'll try with him is the regen build. That honestly looks bonkers.

&amp;#x200B;

Seems [character] will also be more viable if for no other reason than how good the slam boosts make his wave clear.

The problem that I see is that this and last year's laning changes (that plus [Redacted] / [Redacted] led to the stale passive soak solo meta) have flaws that become apparent at high levels of play and those who follow it.

&nbsp;

For everyone else that doesn't play conservatively?  Well things that encourage pushing *do* serve to shake things just like the state goal.  Game gets a lot different when you have to start worrying about coordinated ganks and team movements.  I don't have to in the leagues I play so I go ahead and grab my double globes and push with impunity.  I'll keep an eye on the minimap just in case and it's fine.  That doesn't cut it against really good players who hide their movements or only need an extra hero to gank (which also makes spotting ganks from the minimap harder).

The merc thing is definitely something I've felt before.  There are lots of times where you just stand around and do nothing.  I got a lot better at this game by just being more patient a lot of the times.  Taken to the logical extreme is the staring contest solo's sometimes get when soaking lanes safely.

&amp;#x200B;

A specific map is a good example of always having something to do.  The camps are so frequent and the phase takes you so far away from the lanes that there's just a lot going on.  Conversely you have another map where after the forts are gone it's just a really boring game imo.

None taken, I was more speculating why they keep doing these things.  There's a big tension with pro play being about not taking unnecessary risks and the dev team pushing players to be more aggressive.  But while large parts of the playerbase will follow the intent, pro players just find even nore conservative / passive playstyles (or you can blame [Player A] / [Player B] for being too ungankable for that).

&amp;#x200B;

Maybe it's cyclical though, early in [Tournament Name] it felt like \*every\* solo laner was getting ganked left and right as teams found better rotations to punish them.  They responded by playing extremely conservatively.  So the current solo meta didn't happen immediately after the [Year] laning changes.

It was more the risk that he was going to be suspended for a big chunk of time, turning it to dead money.

